---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ModelEditor from '../components/model/ModelEditor.tsx';
import { archPortalUrl, baPortalUrl, eventStormerUrl } from '../lib/links';

// Server-rendered like `/`, and now for the same reason rather than for
// symmetry: the footer resolves the neighbouring components' addresses from the
// environment, which is configuration read per request rather than baked into
// the image at build time.
const portal = baPortalUrl();
const stormer = eventStormerUrl();
const arch = archPortalUrl();
---

<Layout
	title="Domain model — DDD mapper"
	description="Author the inside of one bounded context as text and see it as a class diagram. Aggregates with the invariants they protect, the entities they own, and the identities they hold across a boundary."
>
	<main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[110rem] px-6 py-10">
		<header class="mb-8">
			<p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.16em] text-ink-muted uppercase dark:text-slate-400">
				ba-hub · ddd mapper
			</p>
			<h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">The inside of one context</h1>
			{/*
				Full width, like the map page's, and rewritten for it: the old line
				was set to `max-w-prose` and wrapped after a third of the screen,
				which put a ragged column above an editor that runs edge to edge.
			*/}
			<p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">
				The <a class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline dark:text-purple-400" href="/">map</a>
				says a bounded context has an aggregate called <code>Submission</code>; this says what
				<code>Submission</code> is — the entities and values it holds, the invariants it keeps
				true across a transaction, and the identities it is allowed to hold across its own
				boundary.
			</p>
		</header>

		<ModelEditor client:only="react" />

		<noscript>
			<div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-6 dark:border-slate-700">
				<h2 class="font-semibold">The editor needs JavaScript</h2>
				<p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">
					A diagram is direct manipulation, which is not something a page can do without
					scripting. The <code>.ddm</code> format is plain text, though, so any editor will do
					— and the <a class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline dark:text-purple-400" href="/dsl#ddm">format page</a>
					documents its grammar.
				</p>
			</div>
		</noscript>

		<footer
			class="mt-10 border-t border-slate-200 pt-6 text-sm text-ink-muted dark:border-slate-700 dark:text-slate-400"
		>
			<p>
				Nothing here is stored on the server — the editor keeps a copy in this browser, and that
				copy goes no further. The <code>.ddm</code> file you export is the model, and it belongs
				beside the <a class="font-semibold text-brand underline" href="/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">map</a> that names the
				context it is the inside of. The practice is written up in the
				<a class="font-semibold text-brand underline" href={portal} target="_blank" rel="noopener">business analysis hub</a>;
				the contexts a model goes one level inside are usually found first on a wall in
				the <a class="font-semibold text-brand underline" href={stormer} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event Stormer</a>, and
				what they get realised as lives in
				the <a class="font-semibold text-brand underline" href={arch} target="_blank" rel="noopener">architecture hub</a>.
				The <a class="font-semibold text-brand underline" href="/dsl#ddm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
					<code>.ddm</code> format is documented</a>.
			</p>
		</footer>
	</main>
</Layout>
